  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nCloudy, dry and mild conditions are prevailing albeit mid to \nhigh level clouds will lower in heights by Monday morning as a \ntrough passes overhead which may bring in some rain shower \nactivity. This feature will drive in better chances of rain \nshower activity than previous forecast packages. Any rain that \nmay be received will be on the lower QPF side if any. A few \nshowers may edge into the area, mainly along and west of the \nOhio River due to high pressure forecast to retreat east\nalthough chances do remain low for this time frame.\n\nModels have come to better agreement on a broad feature \nforecast now to affect the area midweek and into the later part\nof the week. This broad low pressure system will drive in a \ncold frontal boundary late Wednesday and have it pass through on\nThursday. Models have a better handle on the coverage and \nplacement of the system and its progression although they have \ntrouble on timing still. The GFS and EURO have it pushing in \nchances a lot sooner than the Canadian and the NAM. \n\nThe National Blend of Models solution has a lot of weight on \nthe forecast as it raises PoPs for Wednesday and Thursday with \ncategorical associated with frontal passage. Central guidance\nhas also even raised and introduced slight chances for \nthunderstorms in the forecast for Wednesday evening. Due to \nmodel uncertainty of timing, even though we will get a widespread\nrain opportunity, amounts will lack in confidence until we get \ncloser to the event. The main takeaway is that we will get \nbeneficial rainfall amounts, however the timing will lead to \nlarger or smaller amounts as the pace of frontal passage speeds \nup or slows down.\n\nAfter frontal passage there will be a short period of dry \nweather until wrap around flow from the broad upper level \nparent trough swings through the area post frontal passage. \nThis brings back chances for precipitation for the weekend. \nTemperatures will remain above seasonable past midweek then \ncooler air will infiltrate the area allowing temperatures to \nbecome more seasonable for the end of the week and the weekend.",
